## 1. Core Mechanics & Mathematical Principles

### The Skew Formula

Kubernetes calculates topology skew using the formula:

$$\text{Skew} = \text{Count}(\text{Target Topology}) - \text{Min}(\text{Count across all eligible topologies})$$

- **`maxSkew`**: The maximum allowed difference in pod count between any two topology domains.
- If `maxSkew: 1`, and `kube-worker-1` has 2 pods while `kube-worker-2` has 1 pod, the skew is $2 - 1 = 1$ (allowed).
  Placing another pod on `kube-worker-1` would make the skew $3 - 1 = 2$, violating `maxSkew: 1`.

### Key Configuration Directives

| Field | Allowed Values | Purpose & Behavior |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `topologyKey` | `kubernetes.io/hostname`, `topology.kubernetes.io/zone` | Defines domain boundaries for pod balancing. |
| `maxSkew` | Positive integer (e.g., `1`, `2`) | Degree to which pods may be unevenly distributed. |
| `whenUnsatisfiable` | `DoNotSchedule` or `ScheduleAnyway` | Action when placing a pod would exceed `maxSkew`. |
| `labelSelector` | Standard label selector | Finds matching pods that count toward skew calculation. |
| `matchLabelKeys` | `[]string` (e.g., `["pod-template-hash"]`) | Isolates pods during rolling updates (v1.27+ GA). |
| `nodeAffinityPolicy` | `Honor` or `Ignore` | Determines whether node affinity affects topology domains. |
| `nodeTaintsPolicy` | `Honor` or `Ignore` | Ignores tainted nodes from the minimum pod count baseline. |

